  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>
    Most personal trading code is a spaghetti script: strategy logic, broker calls, and
    risk checks tangled together, where one bug costs real money and adding a second
    strategy means rewriting the first. I wanted a platform, not a bot.
  </p>

  <h2>The constraints</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Real money is on the other side of the API, so safety cannot be a convention, it has to be enforced in code.</li>
    <li>Strategies come and go with ideas and elections; the engine should never change when one does.</li>
    <li>Learning requires running the whole thing with no keys, no network, and no packages installed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What it does</h2>
  <p>
    A strategy-agnostic engine, plug-in strategy bundles, and a backtesting harness
    that enforces rigor, written in Python against Alpaca. Paper trading by default;
    live trading requires two separate gates. Without keys it degrades into a dry-run
    offline mode, so a bare checkout runs end-to-end immediately.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it's built this way</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The contract is sacred.</strong> The engine knows nothing about any strategy; a strategy knows nothing about plumbing. No strategy module talks to the broker, and the risk manager has zero strategy-specific branches. Adding a strategy is one new bundle plus one line in config.</li>
    <li><strong>Safety is structural, not disciplined.</strong> A kill-switch halts all trading at a fixed daily loss, a single-order size cap catches sizing bugs, every order carries an idempotency key, and a reconciliation pass compares the book against the broker with an objection window before acting.</li>
    <li><strong>Every decision is logged.</strong> A structured audit log records signals, decisions, fills, and risk rejections, so any position can be explained after the fact.</li>
    <li><strong>Honesty over cosmetics.</strong> Strategies that lack a verified data feed are OFF and say why, with the missing piece documented as a TODO, instead of trading on vibes.</li>
    <li><strong>Serious numbers, silly face.</strong> The same state renders twice: a grey operations dashboard, and a farm where land is capital, crops are positions, frost is the stop-loss, and the gates are the kill-switch. Watching risk is easier when the risk moos at you.</li>
  </ul>

  <details class="tech-details">
    <summary>Technical details</summary>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Python, stdlib-first.</strong> The core runs with zero third-party packages: no installs, no build step, no supply chain to rot. A bare checkout runs end-to-end in dry-run, which makes the whole platform something you can learn by running, not by reading setup docs.</li>
      <li><strong>Alpaca as the only broker dependency.</strong> Commission-free, paper trading built in, decent API, and imported lazily so the platform works without it. One broker, one adapter, nothing broker-shaped leaks past it.</li>
      <li><strong>Hand-rolled backtester.</strong> Deliberate, not lazy: writing the backtest harness yourself forces the rigor questions (survivorship, look-ahead, slippage) into the open instead of hiding behind a library's defaults. Backtrader stays on the shelf as a cross-check.</li>
      <li><strong>Dashboard = stdlib http.server + vanilla JS/CSS.</strong> Read-only by construction: the same state renders as the grey operations view and the farm view, both served as plain HTML with no framework. The garden is pure CSS animation, pixel-art gradients and emoji livestock.</li>
      <li><strong>Optional AI overseer.</strong> An anthropic-backed narrative layer can comment on the overseer's verdicts, but the verdicts themselves are deterministic and work without it: AI comments, rules decide.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <h2>Where it stands</h2>
  <p class="muted-note">
    Paper trading only. Two strategies run live logic (a defined-risk options sleeve and
    an election-thesis basket whose entry window has closed), two more are built but OFF
    until their data feeds are trustworthy. The snapshots show the platform as it is,
    including reconciliation warnings and an over-deployed field, because those are the
    parts the safety system exists to catch.
  </p>
